A Docker image is a read-only template that defines everything needed to run a container:

The OS base (like Alpine or Ubuntu)
Installed software and dependencies
Configuration files
Environment variables
Startup commands
It’s like a snapshot or recipe for creating containers.

It is stored on your local machine: stored in Docker’s internal storage (usually /var/lib/docker).
On remote registries: like Docker Hub, GitHub Container Registry, or private registries. You can pull images from these or push your own.

It is used to create containers: Each container is an instance of an image.
To share environments: You can distribute your image so others can run the exact same setup.
To ensure consistency: Every container from the same image is identical.

Containers keep running until stopped, regardless of your terminal.
Images are templates for containers, containing everything needed to run your app.
Images live locally and/or in registries, and are used to create containers.